  ["Kubernetes", "What is the role of the API server's admission controllers?", "admission-controllers", "Explain the plugin system.", "**Direct answer:** Admission controllers intercept requests after authentication and authorization. They can mutate (change) or validate (accept/reject) requests. Examples: `PodSecurity`, `NamespaceLifecycle`, `ResourceQuota`.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain the plugin system. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** Admission controllers intercept requests after authentication and authorization. They can mutate (change) or validate (accept/reject) requests. Examples: `PodSecurity`, `NamespaceLifecycle`, `ResourceQuota`. The practical takeaway is to explain not only what the resource or command does, but also when you would choose it and what operational trade-off it introduces.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: Admission controllers intercept requests after authentication and authorization. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “How does Kubernetes handle API versioning?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "How does Kubernetes handle API versioning?", "api-versioning", "Explain the versioning strategy.", "**Direct answer:** Kubernetes API has multiple versions (e.g., `v1`, `v1beta1`). The API server supports multiple versions. Resources are converted between versions using conversion webhooks. The preferred version is used for storage.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain the versioning strategy. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** Kubernetes API has multiple versions (e.g., `v1`, `v1beta1`). The API server supports multiple versions. Resources are converted between versions using conversion webhooks. The preferred version is used for storage. The practical takeaway is to explain not only what the resource or command does, but also when you would choose it and what operational trade-off it introduces.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: Kubernetes API has multiple versions (e. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “What is the difference between `kubectl apply` and `kubectl create`?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "What is the difference between `kubectl apply` and `kubectl create`?", "apply-vs-create", "Compare the commands.", "**Direct answer:** `kubectl create` is primarily an imperative create operation: it creates a resource and normally fails if that resource already exists. `kubectl apply` is a declarative operation that creates or updates a resource from configuration, using patching and field ownership mechanisms depending on the apply mode. Modern Kubernetes also supports Server-Side Apply, where the API server tracks field ownership. In production, the key distinction is desired-state management versus a one-time create operation.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Compare the commands. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** `kubectl create` is primarily an imperative create operation: it creates a resource and normally fails if that resource already exists. `kubectl apply` is a declarative operation that creates or updates a resource from configuration, using patching and field ownership mechanisms depending on the apply mode. Modern Kubernetes also supports Server-Side Apply, where the API server tracks field ownership. In production, the key distinction is desired-state management versus a one-time create operation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: `kubectl create` is primarily an imperative create operation: it creates a resource and normally fails if that resource already exists. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “What is the role of the `kube-controller-manager`?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "What is the role of the `kube-controller-manager`?", "kube-controller-manager", "Explain the component.", "**Direct answer:** It is a control plane component that runs multiple controllers (e.g., deployment, replicaset, node). Each controller reconciles the current state to the desired state.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain the component. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** It is a control plane component that runs multiple controllers (e.g., deployment, replicaset, node). Each controller reconciles the current state to the desired state. The practical takeaway is to explain not only what the resource or command does, but also when you would choose it and what operational trade-off it introduces.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: It is a control plane component that runs multiple controllers (e. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “How does the Kubernetes scheduler prioritize nodes?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "How does the Kubernetes scheduler prioritize nodes?", "scheduler-prioritization", "Explain scoring.", "**Direct answer:** The scheduler uses a set of scoring plugins (e.g., `NodeResourcesFit`, `NodeAffinity`, `PodTopologySpread`) to rank nodes. The node with the highest aggregate score is selected.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain scoring. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** The scheduler uses a set of scoring plugins (e.g., `NodeResourcesFit`, `NodeAffinity`, `PodTopologySpread`) to rank nodes. The node with the highest aggregate score is selected. The key interview distinction is the separation between the control plane, which makes decisions and stores desired state, and worker-node components, which execute workloads.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: The scheduler uses a set of scoring plugins (e. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “What is the difference between a node failure and a Pod eviction?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "What is the difference between a node failure and a Pod eviction?", "node-failure-vs-eviction", "Explain the causes.", "**Direct answer:** A node failure is a node going down (e.g., power loss). Pods on that node are considered failed and will be recreated elsewhere. A Pod eviction is a deliberate process where the node evicts Pods due to resource pressure or node maintenance.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain the causes. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** A node failure is a node going down (e.g., power loss). Pods on that node are considered failed and will be recreated elsewhere. A Pod eviction is a deliberate process where the node evicts Pods due to resource pressure or node maintenance. The practical takeaway is to explain not only what the resource or command does, but also when you would choose it and what operational trade-off it introduces.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: A node failure is a node going down (e. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “How do you perform a blue-green deployment in Kubernetes?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "How do you perform a blue-green deployment in Kubernetes?", "blue-green-k8s", "Explain the strategy.", "**Direct answer:** Use two separate Deployments (blue and green) and a Service with a selector. Initially, the service points to blue. To switch, update the service's selector to point to green. This is a simple way to do blue-green.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain the strategy. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** Use two separate Deployments (blue and green) and a Service with a selector. Initially, the service points to blue. To switch, update the service's selector to point to green. This is a simple way to do blue-green. The practical takeaway is to explain not only what the resource or command does, but also when you would choose it and what operational trade-off it introduces.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: Use two separate Deployments (blue and green) and a Service with a selector. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “How does Kubernetes handle container image pull secrets?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "How does Kubernetes handle container image pull secrets?", "image-pull-secrets", "Explain private registry access.", "**Direct answer:** Create a Secret with type `kubernetes.io/dockercfg` or `kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson` containing registry credentials. Then reference the secret in the Pod spec: `imagePullSecrets: [ { name: my-secret } ]`.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain private registry access. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** Create a Secret with type `kubernetes.io/dockercfg` or `kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson` containing registry credentials. Then reference the secret in the Pod spec: `imagePullSecrets: [ { name: my-secret } ]`. The practical takeaway is to explain not only what the resource or command does, but also when you would choose it and what operational trade-off it introduces.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: Create a Secret with type `kubernetes. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “What is the difference between a read-only and a writable volume mount?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "What is the difference between a read-only and a writable volume mount?", "readonly-vs-writable-mount", "Explain mount options.", "**Direct answer:** A volume can be mounted as `readOnly: true` in a container's volume mount. This prevents the container from writing to the volume.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain mount options. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** A volume can be mounted as `readOnly: true` in a container's volume mount. This prevents the container from writing to the volume. The practical takeaway is to explain not only what the resource or command does, but also when you would choose it and what operational trade-off it introduces.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: A volume can be mounted as `readOnly: true` in a container's volume mount. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “How do you enable audit logging in Kubernetes?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "How do you enable audit logging in Kubernetes?", "audit-logging", "Explain configuration.", "**Direct answer:** Enable audit logging by configuring the API server with `--audit-policy-file` and `--audit-log-path`. Define an audit policy to specify what to log. Logs can be sent to a file, webhook, or external system (e.g., ELK).\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain configuration. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** Enable audit logging by configuring the API server with `--audit-policy-file` and `--audit-log-path`. Define an audit policy to specify what to log. Logs can be sent to a file, webhook, or external system (e.g., ELK). The practical takeaway is to explain not only what the resource or command does, but also when you would choose it and what operational trade-off it introduces.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: Enable audit logging by configuring the API server with `--audit-policy-file` and `--audit-log-path`. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “What is the difference between a Service and an Endpoint?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "What is the difference between a Service and an Endpoint?", "service-vs-endpoint", "Explain the relationship.", "**Direct answer:** A Service defines a stable virtual endpoint and selects the backends that should receive traffic. Endpoint information describes the addresses and ports that represent those backends. In modern Kubernetes, EndpointSlice is the scalable API used to represent sets of network endpoints, while the older Endpoints API is a legacy mechanism. Therefore, an interviewer may expect you to mention EndpointSlice when discussing current Kubernetes service backend discovery.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain the relationship. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** A Service defines a stable virtual endpoint and selects the backends that should receive traffic. Endpoint information describes the addresses and ports that represent those backends. In modern Kubernetes, EndpointSlice is the scalable API used to represent sets of network endpoints, while the older Endpoints API is a legacy mechanism. Therefore, an interviewer may expect you to mention EndpointSlice when discussing current Kubernetes service backend discovery.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: A Service defines a stable virtual endpoint and selects the backends that should receive traffic. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “How does Kubernetes handle container liveness and readiness probes?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "How does Kubernetes handle container liveness and readiness probes?", "liveness-readiness-probes", "Explain probes.", "**Direct answer:** A liveness probe answers whether a container should be considered unhealthy enough to restart. A readiness probe answers whether the Pod should currently receive Service traffic. A failed readiness probe removes the Pod from the set of ready Service endpoints without necessarily restarting the container. A failed liveness probe can cause the kubelet to restart the container. Probes should test meaningful application health rather than simply checking whether a process exists.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain probes. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** A liveness probe answers whether a container should be considered unhealthy enough to restart. A readiness probe answers whether the Pod should currently receive Service traffic. A failed readiness probe removes the Pod from the set of ready Service endpoints without necessarily restarting the container. A failed liveness probe can cause the kubelet to restart the container. Probes should test meaningful application health rather than simply checking whether a process exists.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: A liveness probe answers whether a container should be considered unhealthy enough to restart. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “What is the startup probe and when would you use it?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "What is the startup probe and when would you use it?", "startup-probe", "Explain the probe.", "**Direct answer:** A startup probe is designed for containers that need significant time to initialize. While the startup probe has not succeeded, Kubernetes does not run the container's liveness and readiness probes. Once startup succeeds, the normal liveness and readiness checks take over. This prevents a slow-starting application from being repeatedly restarted by an aggressive liveness probe during initialization.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain the probe. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** A startup probe is designed for containers that need significant time to initialize. While the startup probe has not succeeded, Kubernetes does not run the container's liveness and readiness probes. Once startup succeeds, the normal liveness and readiness checks take over. This prevents a slow-starting application from being repeatedly restarted by an aggressive liveness probe during initialization.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: A startup probe is designed for containers that need significant time to initialize. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “How do you create a multi-container Pod and why would you use one?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "How do you create a multi-container Pod and why would you use one?", "multi-container-pod", "Explain the use case.", "**Direct answer:** A multi-container Pod contains multiple containers that share the same network and storage. Use cases: sidecar containers (logging, monitoring), init containers (setup), and ambassadors.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain the use case. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** A multi-container Pod contains multiple containers that share the same network and storage. Use cases: sidecar containers (logging, monitoring), init containers (setup), and ambassadors. The practical takeaway is to explain not only what the resource or command does, but also when you would choose it and what operational trade-off it introduces.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: A multi-container Pod contains multiple containers that share the same network and storage. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “What is an init container and how is it different from a regular container?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "What is an init container and how is it different from a regular container?", "init-container", "Explain setup containers.", "**Direct answer:** An init container runs before the main containers and must complete successfully before the main containers start. They are used for setup tasks (e.g., database schema migration).\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain setup containers. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** An init container runs before the main containers and must complete successfully before the main containers start. They are used for setup tasks (e.g., database schema migration). The practical takeaway is to explain not only what the resource or command does, but also when you would choose it and what operational trade-off it introduces.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: An init container runs before the main containers and must complete successfully before the main containers start. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “What is the difference between a PersistentVolume and a StorageClass?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "What is the difference between a PersistentVolume and a StorageClass?", "pv-vs-storageclass", "Explain the distinction.", "**Direct answer:** A PersistentVolume is a piece of storage provisioned by an administrator. A StorageClass defines a policy for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. StorageClass enables automated storage.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain the distinction. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** A PersistentVolume is a piece of storage provisioned by an administrator. A StorageClass defines a policy for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. StorageClass enables automated storage. In practice, keep the application Pod specification decoupled from the underlying storage implementation by consuming a PVC rather than hard-coding infrastructure-specific storage details.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: A PersistentVolume is a piece of storage provisioned by an administrator. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “How do you implement a service mesh in Kubernetes?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "How do you implement a service mesh in Kubernetes?", "service-mesh-implementation", "Explain installation.", "**Direct answer:** Deploy a service mesh (e.g., Istio) which installs sidecar proxies (Envoy) into each Pod. The proxies handle traffic management, security, and observability. Use Istio's `VirtualService` and `DestinationRule` for routing.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain installation. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** Deploy a service mesh (e.g., Istio) which installs sidecar proxies (Envoy) into each Pod. The proxies handle traffic management, security, and observability. Use Istio's `VirtualService` and `DestinationRule` for routing. The practical takeaway is to explain not only what the resource or command does, but also when you would choose it and what operational trade-off it introduces.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: Deploy a service mesh (e. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “What is the difference between an Ingress and a Gateway API?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "What is the difference between an Ingress and a Gateway API?", "ingress-vs-gateway-api", "Compare the two.", "**Direct answer:** Ingress is a stable Kubernetes API for basic HTTP/HTTPS routing, while Gateway API is a newer, more expressive family of APIs designed for richer traffic-management use cases and clearer separation of responsibilities. Gateway API introduces resources such as GatewayClass, Gateway, and Routes, allowing infrastructure and application teams to have more distinct roles. The important distinction is that Gateway API is an extensible successor-style approach for many networking scenarios, not simply a different name for an Ingress Controller.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Compare the two. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** Ingress is a stable Kubernetes API for basic HTTP/HTTPS routing, while Gateway API is a newer, more expressive family of APIs designed for richer traffic-management use cases and clearer separation of responsibilities. Gateway API introduces resources such as GatewayClass, Gateway, and Routes, allowing infrastructure and application teams to have more distinct roles. The important distinction is that Gateway API is an extensible successor-style approach for many networking scenarios, not simply a different name for an Ingress Controller.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: Ingress is a stable Kubernetes API for basic HTTP/HTTPS routing, while Gateway API is a newer, more expressive family of APIs designed for richer traffic-management use cases and clearer separation of responsibilities. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “How do you handle DNS resolution for Services in a cluster?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "How do you handle DNS resolution for Services in a cluster?", "cluster-dns", "Explain CoreDNS.", "**Direct answer:** Kubernetes normally provides cluster DNS through a DNS service such as CoreDNS. The kubelet configures Pod DNS settings so applications can resolve Service names without hard-coding Pod IP addresses. A Service receives DNS records based on its name and namespace, for example `my-service.default.svc.cluster.local`. For troubleshooting, check the Pod's `/etc/resolv.conf`, CoreDNS Pods and logs, the DNS Service, NetworkPolicies, and whether the queried Service actually exists.\n\n**Why this is asked:** Explain CoreDNS. Interviewers are checking whether you understand the Kubernetes concept itself, why it exists, and how you would use it correctly in a production environment.\n\n**Breaking it down:** Kubernetes normally provides cluster DNS through a DNS service such as CoreDNS. The kubelet configures Pod DNS settings so applications can resolve Service names without hard-coding Pod IP addresses. A Service receives DNS records based on its name and namespace, for example `my-service.default.svc.cluster.local`. For troubleshooting, check the Pod's `/etc/resolv.conf`, CoreDNS Pods and logs, the DNS Service, NetworkPolicies, and whether the queried Service actually exists.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:** I would answer this in an interview by starting with the definition: Kubernetes normally provides cluster DNS through a DNS service such as CoreDNS. Then I would explain the main mechanism, give one practical example, and mention the most important limitation or trade-off. For this question, I would emphasize the specific point the interviewer is testing rather than listing unrelated Kubernetes features.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:** A likely follow-up is: “What is Kubernetes and why is it used?” Be ready to connect this answer to the next concept and explain how the two work together in a real cluster."],
  ["Kubernetes", "What is the role of the `kube-proxy` in service discovery?", "kube-proxy-discovery", "Explain its function.", "kube-proxy implements the Kubernetes service abstraction. It maintains network rules (iptables/IPVS) to route traffic to Pods. It is responsible for load balancing and service discovery."]
